WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
React Native upgrades have a reputation.

2
00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:04.600
You set out to bump a version number

3
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:07.600
and suddenly your entire build pipeline collapses

4
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.400
in a time-consuming failure.

5
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:12.800
Your terminal floods with incomprehensible Xcode C++

6
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.100
compiler errors or Gradle crashes out

7
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.300
before the build even properly begins.

8
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:18.500
You look at the stack trace

9
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.900
and none of it points to the JavaScript or TypeScript

10
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:22.100
you actually wrote.

11
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.100
The errors seem entirely disconnected

12
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:25.800
from your application logic.

13
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.300
That's because the vast majority

14
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.100
of these native errors have nothing to do

15
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.700
with code regressions or bad package updates.

16
00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:33.400
These failures are the direct result

17
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.100
of invisible, untracked drift

18
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:36.900
between your local machine's environment

19
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:38.500
and the underlying platform tools

20
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.300
required to compile the app.

21
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.500
When it's time to upgrade,

22
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:42.900
the standard developer instinct

23
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:46.500
is to open package.json and start bumping dependency versions.

24
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:49.100
Executing this step first is a process error.

25
00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:52.200
It guarantees false positive build failures

26
00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.400
because your system isn't ready to compile

27
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.500
the code you're asking it to run.

28
00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.100
To prepare your system,

29
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:01.900
you need the official react native compatibility matrix.

30
00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:05.200
This is the official source of truth for your project.

31
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.000
The matrix dictates the exact version alignment

32
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.600
required across multiple disconnected software layers

33
00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.400
from JavaScript runtimes to native compilers

34
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.300
before a single line of code can compile.

35
00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:20.700
Achieving exact alignment with this compatibility matrix

36
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.700
is the actual first step of any upgrade.

37
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.900
It has to precede any code modifications.

38
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:29.500
This table is the compatibility matrix.

39
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:31.700
It maps every react native release

40
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:35.700
from 0.72 up to 0.84 and beyond

41
00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.500
to its specific environmental dependencies.

42
00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:42.100
It provides minimum required versions for node, JDK,

43
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:45.300
Xcode, cocoa pods, and Android SDKs.

44
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:46.800
Looking at the node column,

45
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.200
notice the jump from node 18 to 20

46
00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.400
at react native 0.80

47
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:54.300
and to node 22 at 0.84.

48
00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.300
If you install node globally using a package manager

49
00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:00.700
like app or brew, you guarantee mismatches.

50
00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:02.200
Different developers on your team

51
00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.200
will end up on different node versions

52
00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.000
leading to localized bugs.

53
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.200
The required solution is to use a local version manager

54
00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.300
like NVM or FNM.

55
00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:13.700
These tools allow you to isolate the node runtime

56
00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.500
on a per project basis.

57
00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.700
You enforce this isolation using an NVM RC file

58
00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:20.000
in your repository.

59
00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.500
This declares the exact node version needed,

60
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.200
forcing your machine and CI to read the same specification.

61
00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:29.200
By committing an NVM RC file to your code base,

62
00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.500
you completely eliminate node-based works

63
00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.600
on my machine discrepancies during your CI pipelines.

64
00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.100
Moving down the matrix brings us

65
00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:38.700
to the most frequent cause of broken CI builds,

66
00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:40.300
the JDK transition.

67
00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:44.100
Between react native 0.72 and 0.73,

68
00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:47.800
the framework shifted from JDK 11 to JDK 17.

69
00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:50.500
This introduces a severe debugging pitfall.

70
00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:54.200
You might check your terminal and see Java 17 installed,

71
00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.000
but the Gradle build tool is still quietly reading

72
00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.200
an older incorrect version.

73
00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:03.800
To fix this, explicitly set your Java home environment variable.

74
00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:06.800
This configuration forces Gradle to route directly

75
00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:09.400
to the JDK 17 directory.

76
00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:11.400
You must mirror this explicit routing

77
00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.000
in GitHub actions workflows by utilizing

78
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.100
the setup-java step.

79
00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.800
This pins the distribution and version.

80
00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:22.100
Without explicitly tethering Gradle to the correct JDK

81
00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:24.000
via the Java home variable,

82
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.000
your Android CI builds will remain inherently non-deterministic.

83
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.700
Shifting focus to the Apple ecosystem

84
00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:33.700
reveals the most rigid dependency chains

85
00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:36.000
in the entire upgrade process.

86
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.500
iOS compilation operates on a strict rule.

87
00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:41.800
Xcode versions are immutably tied

88
00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:44.100
to specific Mac OS versions.

89
00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:49.900
Xcode 15.1 requires Sonoma and 16.1 requires Sequoia.

90
00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.000
This is a critical infrastructure jump

91
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:54.500
for react native 0.81,

92
00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:57.600
strictly mandating Xcode 16.1.

93
00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:01.600
If your YAML configuration requests an older Mac OS runner,

94
00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:04.900
this requirement breaks your CI pipelines immediately.

95
00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:08.700
The build fails before dependencies even begin to install.

96
00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:11.100
Once the operating system is locked in,

97
00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:14.600
you have to manage dependency management via CocoaPods.

98
00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:17.700
Committing your gem file dot lock is mandatory.

99
00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:20.200
It is the only reliable mechanism to pin

100
00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:22.400
the exact CocoaPods version required

101
00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:24.400
across all your environments.

102
00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:26.200
Failing to lock your Mac OS,

103
00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:29.700
your specific Xcode and CocoaPods versions in Unison,

104
00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:32.400
guarantees your iOS builds will fail.

105
00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:35.500
The final critical layer of the compatibility matrix

106
00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:39.500
involves the Android SDK and NDK requirements.

107
00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:43.200
Unlike iOS, which relies on Xcode's internal SDKs,

108
00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.400
Android requires manual version alignment

109
00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:48.000
directly within your project's Gradle files.

110
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:50.300
As you progress through React Native releases,

111
00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:53.200
the min SDK requirements steadily advances,

112
00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:56.700
stepping up from API21 to API24.

113
00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:59.800
Then there is the native development kit or NDK.

114
00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:02.900
The NDK is responsible for compiling C++ code

115
00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:05.100
for React Native's new architecture.

116
00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:07.800
For React Native 0.76 and above,

117
00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:12.000
the framework strictly mandates NDK version R27 or higher.

118
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.200
Ignoring this specific NDK version bump,

119
00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:16.900
guarantees native C++ compilation failures

120
00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:18.500
during the Gradle build phase.

121
00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:22.000
You have to meticulously align these underlying Gradle SDK blocks

122
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.200
to satisfy the framework's complex native compilation engine.

123
00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:27.700
Before you modify a single line of code base code,

124
00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:29.700
you need a systematic method for verifying

125
00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:31.600
all of these matrix parameters.

126
00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:35.000
You need a pre-upgrade environment validation script.

127
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:37.000
You execute this validation protocol

128
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:40.500
on both the developer's local machine and the CI agent.

129
00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:43.400
The order of operations starts at the runtime layer.

130
00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:45.200
The script scans your system paths

131
00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:49.000
and locks in the exact node and JDK versions required.

132
00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:52.100
Next, the validation moves to the iOS environment,

133
00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:55.600
verifying the exact Xcode and cocoa pods pinning.

134
00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:58.000
The final phase scans the manual SDK

135
00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:01.600
and NDK configurations inside the Android Gradle files,

136
00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:04.300
ensuring every digit matches the matrix.

137
00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:07.800
Achieving this fully validated greenlit environment checklist

138
00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:10.600
is the only absolute proof that your system is capable

139
00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:12.700
of running a successful upgrade.

140
00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:16.400
Explicitly pinning all of your system runtimes and native SDKs

141
00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:18.400
is the only reliable way to eliminate

142
00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:20.600
the works on my machine paradox.

143
00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:23.000
The professional rule of upgrades is simple.

144
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:26.000
Identify and resolve every single environment mismatch

145
00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:28.500
against the official matrix before proceeding.

146
00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:31.000
Only when your machine environment aligns

147
00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:33.400
with your target react native version

148
00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:36.200
should you transition to code based tasks.

149
00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:39.300
Opening package.json to alter dependencies

150
00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:41.000
is not your first step.

151
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:44.900
It is the final step of the environment preparation process.

152
00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:47.800
Treating the baseline environment with the same rigor

153
00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:50.900
as the code base itself is the most reliable way

154
00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:53.900
to ensure your upgrades actually succeed.

